In article <32600@mimsy.umd.edu> leonard@mimsy.umd.edu (Leonard Dickens ) writes:
>Charles J. Jones writes:
>>A polymorph wand [should] only
>>affect items that are or were living at one time, i.e. food items,
>>creatures and corpses.  Thereby you could still get those certain armor
>>items with a WoP, but you could really polypile with any useful
>>results.  
>
>main excuse for having polymorph around.  It would be interesting if there 
>were more than just a few useful items obtainable via polymorphing various 
>corpses... maybe:
> a cloak of invisibility	from	an invisible stalker corpse
> gauntlets of giant strength	from	2 giant corpses
> a cloak of displacement	from	2 displacer beast corpses (see D&D)
> guantlets of dexterity 	from	2 leprechaun corpses
> a cloak of protection		from    2 vampire corpses
> an elven cloak			from    2 mimic corpses
> a cloak of magic resistance	from	2 cockatrice corpses
>This would be a way to increase the probability that these powerful and 
>useful items enter the game, without giving them away.

Great idea. I like it a lot. Maybe what should also be added to the WoP trick
is to generate only less usefull items when zapping a WoP on a pile, so after
a few zaps you end up with only rocks, scrolls of scare monster, potions of
water, wand of nothings, darts and fedora's, as to me it seems that
polymorphing should decrease the intrinsic value/abitlity of the item as this
'energy' is already used to transform the item. It is quite hard to understand
that a mummy wrapping could be transformed into a crystal plate mail, while
the other way around seems more natural.
